{"slug":"caro-kann-foundations-d1","studyId":"yt-Jiz7KLKjLas-caro-kann-foundations-d1","title":"The main lines at a glance","category":"openings","openingFamilies":["Caro-Kann Defense"],"color":"black","level":"beginner","author":"Hanging Pawns","authorUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/@HangingPawns","studyUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jiz7KLKjLas","source":{"kind":"youtube","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jiz7KLKjLas","channel":"Hanging Pawns","channelUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/@HangingPawns","title":"Caro-Kann Defense (introduction, main variations & ideas) ⎸Chess Openings"},"description":"Play Black through the Classical ...Bf5 main line, the Karpov ...Nd7, the Korchnoi ...Nf6 and the Bronstein-Larsen, the way Stjepan lays them out.","permissionNote":"Lesson for a day of the Caro-Kann Foundations course; the course permission (pending) governs it.","chapters":[{"id":"chapter-1","name":"1...c6 and the Classical 4...Bf5"},{"id":"chapter-2","name":"The main line: provoking c3 and c4"},{"id":"chapter-3","name":"The Karpov Variation: 4...Nd7"},{"id":"chapter-4","name":"4...Nf6: the Korchnoi Variation"},{"id":"chapter-5","name":"If you choose the Bronstein-Larsen"}],"chapterData":[{"id":"chapter-1","name":"1...c6 and the Classical 4...Bf5","url":"","annotator":null,"orientation":"black","startFen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","gamebook":false,"moves":[{"id":"n0","san":"e4","uci":"e2e4","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1","children":[{"id":"n1","san":"c6","uci":"c7c6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2p5/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","children":[{"id":"n2","san":"d4","uci":"d2d4","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2p5/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2","children":[{"id":"n3","san":"d5","uci":"d7d5","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3","children":[{"id":"n4","san":"Nc3","uci":"b1c3","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/3PP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 3","children":[{"id":"n5","san":"dxe4","uci":"d5e4","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/8/3Pp3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4","children":[{"id":"n6","san":"Nxe4","uci":"c3e4","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4","children":[{"id":"n7","san":"Bf5","uci":"c8f5","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/5b2/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 1 5","children":[{"id":"n8","san":"Ng3","uci":"e4g3","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/5b2/3P4/6N1/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 2 5","children":[{"id":"n9","san":"Bg6","uci":"f5g6","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p3b1/8/3P4/6N1/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 3 6","children":[{"id":"n10","san":"h4","uci":"h2h4","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p3b1/8/3P3P/6N1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 6","children":[{"id":"n11","san":"h6","uci":"h7h6","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2ppp1/2p3bp/8/3P3P/6N1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 7","children":[{"id":"n12","san":"Nf3","uci":"g1f3","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2ppp1/2p3bp/8/3P3P/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 1 7","children":[{"id":"n13","san":"Nd7","uci":"b8d7","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p3bp/8/3P3P/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 8","children":[{"id":"n14","san":"h5","uci":"h4h5","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p3bp/7P/3P4/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 8","children":[{"id":"n15","san":"Bh7","uci":"g6h7","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1npppb/2p4p/7P/3P4/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 1 9","children":[{"id":"n16","san":"Bd3","uci":"f1d3","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1npppb/2p4p/7P/3P4/3B1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 2 9","children":[{"id":"n17","san":"Bxd3","uci":"h7d3","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p4p/7P/3P4/3b1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 0 10","children":[{"id":"n18","san":"Qxd3","uci":"d1d3","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p4p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 0 10","children":[],"comment":"White argues he has better development and more control of the center. In exchange, h4 and h5 weakened his kingside, so White can never really castle there.","ts":200}],"comment":"Black doesn't really have an alternative to the trade.","ts":192},{"id":"n19","san":"Ngf6","uci":"g8f6","fen":"r2qkb1r/pp1npppb/2p2n1p/7P/3P4/3B1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 10","children":[]},{"id":"n20","san":"Ndf6","uci":"d7f6","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp2pppb/2p2n1p/7P/3P4/3B1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 10","children":[]}],"comment":"By far White's most common move here, with over 20,000 games: trade off Black's light-squared bishop, which has moved several times, for one that moved once.","ts":184}]}],"comment":"h5 again threatens nothing less than the bishop.","ts":173}],"comment":"The most principled continuation. Black develops the knight to d7 first.","ts":414},{"id":"n21","san":"Nf6","uci":"g8f6","fen":"rn1qkb1r/pp2ppp1/2p2nbp/8/3P3P/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 8","children":[],"comment":"Stjepan calls ...Nf6 a mistake here because Ne5 gives White a lot of activity, though the engine rates it close to ...Nd7. Play his move: develop the knight to d7 first.","ts":414},{"id":"n22","san":"e6","uci":"e7e6","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp3pp1/2p1p1bp/8/3P3P/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 8","children":[]},{"id":"n23","san":"Bh7","uci":"g6h7","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppb/2p4p/8/3P3P/5NN1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 8","children":[]}]}],"comment":"Making room for the bishop. h5 would be inferior: it weakens too many squares and gives the knight on g3 and the queen a clear target on the h-file.","ts":159}],"comment":"White's idea is to exploit the bishop on g6, which is slightly misplaced and has already used a couple of tempi. h4 threatens h5, trapping it.","ts":143},{"id":"n24","san":"Bd3","uci":"f1d3","fen":"rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p3b1/8/3P4/3B2N1/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 4 6","children":[],"comment":"White doesn't have to start with h4. Nf3, Be3 or Bd3, which challenges the bishop at once, all avoid weakening the kingside and might surprise your opponent.","ts":397}],"comment":"Saving the bishop. This is the starting position of the Caro-Kann main line.","ts":135}]}],"comment":"The main move, challenging the knight on e4.","ts":126},{"id":"n25","san":"Nd7","uci":"b8d7","fen":"r1bqkbnr/pp1npppp/2p5/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 1 5","children":[],"comment":"The Karpov Variation, a very good option: it has its own chapter. Here, find the main move.","ts":471},{"id":"n26","san":"Nf6","uci":"g8f6","fen":"rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/2p2n2/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 1 5","children":[],"comment":"The second most popular move, with its own chapters. Here, find the main move.","ts":678},{"id":"n27","san":"g6","uci":"g7g6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pp1p/2p3p1/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5","children":[]},{"id":"n28","san":"h6","uci":"h7h6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2ppp1/2p4p/8/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5","children":[]},{"id":"n29","san":"a5","uci":"a7a5","fen":"rnbqkbnr/1p2pppp/2p5/p7/3PN3/8/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5","children":[]}]}],"comment":"From here Black really has only one move: take on e4.","ts":118},{"id":"n30","san":"e6","uci":"e7e6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp3ppp/2p1p3/3p4/3PP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4","children":[]},{"id":"n31","san":"h6","uci":"h7h6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2ppp1/2p4p/3p4/3PP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4","children":[]},{"id":"n32","san":"a6","uci":"a7a6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/1p2pppp/p1p5/3p4/3PP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4","children":[]}]},{"id":"n33","san":"e5","uci":"e4e5","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3pP3/3P4/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3","children":[],"comment":"White has a couple of other replies after d5, and this is where the lines branch out. The Advance, e5, gets its own day.","ts":102}],"comment":"In every line Black meets d4 with d5, challenging the center. That's the point of c6: instead of e5 or c5, Black prepares d5 and fights for the center one move later.","ts":69},{"id":"n34","san":"g6","uci":"g7g6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp1ppp1p/2p3p1/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3","children":[]},{"id":"n35","san":"d6","uci":"d7d6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2pp4/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3","children":[]},{"id":"n36","san":"a6","uci":"a7a6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/1p1ppppp/p1p5/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3","children":[]},{"id":"n37","san":"e6","uci":"e7e6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2p1p3/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3","children":[]}],"comment":"White has three moves here, and d4 is by far the most common.","ts":52}],"comment":"This is the Caro-Kann. Whatever happens next, c6 is there to support a later d5.","ts":44},{"id":"n38","san":"e5","uci":"e7e5","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","children":[]},{"id":"n39","san":"c5","uci":"c7c5","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","children":[]},{"id":"n40","san":"e6","uci":"e7e6","fen":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/4p3/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","children":[]},{"id":"n41","san":"Nc6","uci":"b8c6","fen":"r1bqkbnr/pppppppp/2n5/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 2","children":[]}]}],"intro":"Stjepan starts with the basics: the opening principles and the main line, played move by move. You have Black.","introTs":36},{"id":"chapter-2","name":"The main line: provoking c3 and c4","url":"","annotator":null,"orientation":"black","startFen":"r2qkbnr/pp1nppp1/2p4p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 0 10","gamebook":false,"moves":[{"id":"n0","san":"e6","uci":"e7e6","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R1B1K2R w KQkq - 0 11","children":[{"id":"n1","san":"Bf4","uci":"c1f4","fen":"r2qkbnr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/7P/3P1B2/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R3K2R b KQkq - 1 11","children":[{"id":"n2","san":"Qa5+","uci":"d8a5","fen":"r3kbnr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/q6P/3P1B2/3Q1NN1/PPP2PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 2 12","children":[{"id":"n3","san":"Bd2","uci":"f4d2","fen":"r3kbnr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/q6P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPPB1PP1/R3K2R b KQkq - 3 12","children":[{"id":"n4","san":"Bb4","uci":"f8b4","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/q6P/1b1P4/3Q1NN1/PPPB1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 4 13","children":[{"id":"n5","san":"c3","uci":"c2c3","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/q6P/1b1P4/2PQ1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R b KQkq - 0 13","children":[{"id":"n6","san":"Be7","uci":"b4e7","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1nbpp1/2p1p2p/q6P/3P4/2PQ1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 14","children":[{"id":"n7","san":"c4","uci":"c3c4","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1nbpp1/2p1p2p/q6P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R b KQkq - 0 14","children":[{"id":"n8","san":"Qc7","uci":"a5c7","fen":"r3k1nr/ppqnbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 15","children":[{"id":"n9","san":"O-O-O","uci":"e1c1","fen":"r3k1nr/ppqnbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R b kq - 2 15","children":[{"id":"n10","san":"Ngf6","uci":"g8f6","fen":"r3k2r/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R w kq - 3 16","children":[{"id":"n11","san":"Kb1","uci":"c1b1","fen":"r3k2r/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R b kq - 4 16","children":[{"id":"n12","san":"O-O","uci":"e8g8","fen":"r4rk1/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w - - 5 17","children":[],"comment":"Black castles: the starting position of the main line. Engines give Black about half a pawn, partly because the h5 pawn is overextended and the knight on g3 is fairly useless. Practically it's roughly level, but Black's king is safer and his queenside attack is ready-made.","ts":305},{"id":"n13","san":"Rd8","uci":"a8d8","fen":"3rk2r/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w k - 5 17","children":[]},{"id":"n14","san":"a5","uci":"a7a5","fen":"r3k2r/1pqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/p6P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w kq - 0 17","children":[]},{"id":"n15","san":"b5","uci":"b7b5","fen":"r3k2r/p1qnbpp1/2p1pn1p/1p5P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w kq - 0 17","children":[]},{"id":"n16","san":"a6","uci":"a7a6","fen":"r3k2r/1pqnbpp1/p1p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w kq - 0 17","children":[]},{"id":"n17","san":"Ng4","uci":"f6g4","fen":"r3k2r/ppqnbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP2n1/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w kq - 5 17","children":[]},{"id":"n18","san":"Rb8","uci":"a8b8","fen":"1r2k2r/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w k - 5 17","children":[]}]}]},{"id":"n19","san":"Rd8","uci":"a8d8","fen":"3rk1nr/ppqnbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R w k - 3 16","children":[]},{"id":"n20","san":"b5","uci":"b7b5","fen":"r3k1nr/p1qnbpp1/2p1p2p/1p5P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R w kq - 0 16","children":[]},{"id":"n21","san":"a6","uci":"a7a6","fen":"r3k1nr/1pqnbpp1/p1p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R w kq - 0 16","children":[]},{"id":"n22","san":"Rc8","uci":"a8c8","fen":"2r1k1nr/ppqnbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/2KR3R w k - 3 16","children":[]}],"comment":"White most commonly castles queenside, and the king will step to b1. Kingside castling is too risky after the h-pawn's advance.","ts":291}],"comment":"Black spent only a couple of moves and provoked c3, c4 and the bishop's retreat from f4 to d2. A favorable version for Black, even if White keeps a slight opening edge.","ts":270},{"id":"n23","san":"Qb6","uci":"a5b6","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1nbpp1/1qp1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 15","children":[]},{"id":"n24","san":"Qa6","uci":"a5a6","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1nbpp1/q1p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 15","children":[]},{"id":"n25","san":"Qd8","uci":"a5d8","fen":"r2qk1nr/pp1nbpp1/2p1p2p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 15","children":[]}],"comment":"Now c4 attacks the queen: the bishop on d2 sees a5 again.","ts":266}],"comment":"The bishop simply retreats. It could have gone straight from f8 to e7, so Black lost no time, and this way it provoked c3.","ts":258},{"id":"n26","san":"Bd6","uci":"b4d6","fen":"r3k1nr/pp1n1pp1/2pbp2p/q6P/3P4/2PQ1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 14","children":[]},{"id":"n27","san":"Bf8","uci":"b4f8","fen":"r3kbnr/pp1n1pp1/2p1p2p/q6P/3P4/2PQ1NN1/PP1B1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 14","children":[]}],"comment":"White answers with c3, another queenside weakening.","ts":254}],"comment":"Challenging the bishop once again.","ts":250},{"id":"n28","san":"Qc7","uci":"a5c7","fen":"r3kbnr/ppqn1pp1/2p1p2p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPPB1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 4 13","children":[]},{"id":"n29","san":"Qb6","uci":"a5b6","fen":"r3kbnr/pp1n1pp1/1qp1p2p/7P/3P4/3Q1NN1/PPPB1PP1/R3K2R w KQkq - 4 13","children":[]}]}],"comment":"Here's one of Black's ideas in the main line: force White to wea